WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:06.546
An FX quote flow emits a cached rate, then calls a provider.

2
00:00:06.546 --> 00:00:12.202
A catch below it emits a fallback rate, and the collector writes each rate to the ledger.

3
00:00:12.202 --> 00:00:14.935
Picture a pipe, with the source at the top.

4
00:00:15.067 --> 00:00:17.334
The provider throws an IOException.

5
00:00:17.334 --> 00:00:22.257
It falls from above, lands in the net, and catch emits the fallback instead.

6
00:00:22.257 --> 00:00:23.812
The caller sees nothing.

7
00:00:23.933 --> 00:00:29.935
A withTimeout that fires up there lands in the net too, even though it is a CancellationException.

8
00:00:30.067 --> 00:00:31.843
Now the ledger write fails.

9
00:00:31.843 --> 00:00:33.619
That happens below the net.

10
00:00:33.619 --> 00:00:35.527
Does catch emit the fallback?

11
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:38.180
No.

12
00:00:38.180 --> 00:00:42.435
The rock was dropped below the net, so it falls straight to the caller.

13
00:00:42.435 --> 00:00:47.769
catch can tell, because it remembers every exception that comes back out of its own emit.

14
00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:53.565
One thing from above passes the net on purpose: the cancellation of your own job.

15
00:00:53.565 --> 00:00:56.243
A caller that leaves stops the whole pipe.

16
00:00:57.333 --> 00:00:59.342
Now swap the net for retry(3).

17
00:00:59.342 --> 00:01:03.561
The settlement feed sends payment 1 and payment 2, then resets.

18
00:01:03.561 --> 00:01:05.503
What does the ledger receive?

19
00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:12.572
A retry collects the upstream again from the top, like a rewound tape.

20
00:01:12.572 --> 00:01:15.446
Payments 1 and 2 play again, then payment 3.

21
00:01:15.446 --> 00:01:20.607
Two payments are posted twice, so the ledger must skip ids it has already seen.

22
00:01:21.467 --> 00:01:24.810
Last, a provider that has stopped answering.

23
00:01:24.810 --> 00:01:33.321
Each call gets 300 milliseconds, the retry waits 100, 200 and 400 between attempts, and then a fallback answers.

24
00:01:33.321 --> 00:01:34.841
When does it answer?

25
00:01:37.333 --> 00:01:43.511
Calls start at 0, 400, 900 and 1600, and each is cut at 300.

26
00:01:43.511 --> 00:01:49.997
At 1900 the primary gives up, and the fallback answers at 1930.

27
00:01:50.067 --> 00:01:53.683
Each timeout falls from above, so the retry sees it.

28
00:01:53.683 --> 00:01:59.595
But a timeout is a cancellation, not an IOException, so your retry rule must list it.

29
00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:04.289
Here is the chain in Kotlin, with the Reactor version below it.

30
00:02:07.067 --> 00:02:10.871
So catch and retry only handle what falls from above them.

31
00:02:10.871 --> 00:02:13.757
And a retry replays the stream from the top.

32
00:02:13.867 --> 00:02:14.691
Quick check.

33
00:02:14.691 --> 00:02:17.647
catch sits above a map, and the map throws.

34
00:02:17.647 --> 00:02:19.433
Who handles the exception?
